  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The `BuggyToast` component fails to communicate its existence or content to assistive technology, lacks keyboard operability, and provides no pause/cancel mechanism for auto-dismissal. While it fulfills basic visual rendering and timer logic, it violates core WAI-ARIA APG Toast/Status patterns and WCAG 4.1.3. It cannot ship in this state.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\nBased on the component type (dynamic toast/notification), I predicted:\n1. Missing `aria-live` / `role=\"status\"` or `alert` for screen reader announcement.\n2. No focus management or keyboard dismissal handler.\n3. Auto-dismiss timer without pause/cancel control causing cognitive friction.\n4. Visual-only state indicators (success/error) not programmatically exposed.\n5. Potential focus loss on unmount if a user somehow interacts with it.\n\nAll 5 predictions materialized as actual findings during code review.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Missing `aria-live` region for screen reader announcement**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyToast.tsx:19-21` renders `<div className=\"toast-notification\">{message}</div>` with no ARIA attributes or live region role.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages; WAI-ARIA APG Toast pattern requires `role=\"status\"` (polite) or `role=\"alert\"` (assertive) + `aria-live`.\n   - Fix: Wrap the message in a live region: `<div role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic=\"true\" className=\"toast-notification\">{message}</div>`. Use `role=\"alert\"` if the toast conveys urgent/error state.\n\n2. **No keyboard dismissal or focus management**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyToast.tsx:19-21` contains no interactive elements, event listeners for `Escape`, or focus handlers. The component unmounts via `return null` when `isVisible` becomes false.\n   - User group impacted: Keyboard-only users, motor-impaired users\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), 2.1.2 (No Keyboard Trap), APG Toast pattern requires keyboard dismissibility (typically `Escape` or `Enter`) and safe unmount behavior.\n   - Fix: Add a dismiss button (`<button aria-label=\"Dismiss notification\">`) inside the toast with an `onClick` that sets `isVisible(false)`. Ensure the button is focusable and visible on hover/focus. If the toast is purely informational, document that it's non-interactive but still announce it via `aria-live`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Auto-dismiss timer lacks pause/cancel mechanism**\n   - Evidence: `BuggyToast.tsx:6-12` uses a fixed `setTimeout` with no user control to pause, extend, or cancel the dismissal.\n   - User group impacted: Cognitive accessibility users, low vision users reading slowly\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 2.2.2 (Pause, Stop, Hide) applies to auto-playing content >3s; cognitive UX guidelines require user control over transient UI. APG recommends providing a close/dismiss action for all toasts.\n   - Fix: Add a visible \"Close\" or \"Dismiss\" button. If the toast is strictly informational and non-interactive, add `aria-live=\"polite\"` but document that it auto-dismisses, and consider extending duration or adding a subtle pause-on-hover/focus indicator.\n\n2. **Visual type indicators not implemented in code**\n   - Evidence: Expected behavior states \"Visual indicator shows toast type (success, error, etc.)\", but the component only accepts a `message` string. No `type` prop, no conditional classes/ARIA, no icon handling.\n   - User group impacted: Low vision users, color-blind users, screen reader users\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 1.4.1 (Use of Color), 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n   - Fix: Accept a `type` prop (`'success' | 'error' | 'warning' | 'info'`). Map it to conditional ARIA roles (`role=\"alert\"` for error, `role=\"status\"` for others) and visual classes. Decorative icons must use `aria-hidden=\"true\"`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Missing `aria-atomic` on live region**: Without `aria-atomic=\"true\"`, screen readers may announce partial updates if the toast content changes dynamically. Add it alongside `aria-live`.\n- **Focus loss on unmount**: If a user somehow focuses an element inside the toast before auto-dismiss, React's `return null` will drop focus to `document.body`. Wrap unmount in a safe focus restoration or ensure the toast is non-focusable until dismissed.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Consider using a toast container/manager pattern rather than mounting individual toasts directly. This centralizes live region management and prevents multiple concurrent announcements from competing for AT attention.\n- Add `lang` attribute if messages contain foreign language text.\n- Ensure the toast's visual position (e.g., top-right) doesn't obscure focused elements on small viewports (WCAG 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured).\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Live region scope**: No container manages multiple toasts. Concurrent toasts will cause announcement collisions or overwrite each other in the accessibility tree.\n- **Dismissal state sync**: `isVisible` toggles via timer but has no explicit dismissal event tied to user action, creating a single-path interaction model.\n- **Semantic wrapper**: A `<div>` with only a class name provides zero semantic meaning to AT. Toasts should be wrapped in a landmark or explicitly scoped live region.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | No `aria-live`/`role`; toast is invisible to AT |\n| Keyboard-only | HIGH | No dismiss button, no `Escape` handler, focus drops on unmount |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Depends on visual styling (not provided); focus indicator missing |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Auto-dismiss without pause/cancel; no type indication programmatically |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations/transitions in code |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No audio components |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Visual-only type indicators (if added later) must meet text/UI contrast thresholds |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails at the foundational level of assistive technology communication. A toast that does not announce itself to screen readers is functionally broken for blind users. Combined with zero keyboard dismissal and no pause mechanism, it violates multiple WCAG criteria and APG patterns. The design requires structural changes (live region implementation, interactive dismiss control, type prop handling) before it can ship. Verdict upgraded from REVISE to REJECT due to complete AT communication failure.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- How will multiple concurrent toasts be managed? Will they stack in a single `aria-live` container or render as independent regions?\n- Is this toast intended to be dismissible by the user, or strictly informational? This dictates whether `role=\"alert\"`/`role=\"status\"` + button vs. passive live region is appropriate.\n- What is the visual styling for success/error types? If color-only indicators are used, they will fail WCAG 1.4.1 without supplementary text/iconography.",
